+\sectiondiv{2}{Newton's Laws of Motion}{1st Law: inertia \textbullet{} 2nd Law: $F = ma$ \textbullet{} 3rd Law: action--reaction}
+
+\readingTwo
+
+
+\subsection{Newton's First Law --- The Law of Inertia}
+
+\diagNewtonOne
+\figcap{Figure 2.1 --- Newton's First Law}
+
+\begin{infobox}{First Law}
+\textit{``An object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion at constant velocity, unless acted upon by an unbalanced (net) force.''}
+
+\medskip
+\textbf{Inertia} is the tendency of an object to resist changes to its motion. Greater mass $=$ greater inertia.
+\end{infobox}
+
+\begin{yr7box}
+\question{1}{2}{Explain why passengers lurch forward when a bus suddenly brakes. Use the words \textit{inertia} and \textit{unbalanced force} in your answer.}
+\answerbox{3cm}
+
+\question{2}{2}{A hockey puck slides across an almost frictionless ice surface. What will eventually stop it, and why?}
+\answerbox{2.5cm}
+\end{yr7box}
+
+\subsection{Newton's Second Law --- $F = ma$}
+
+\diagFma
+\figcap{Figure 2.2 --- Newton's Second Law: force, mass and acceleration}
+
+\begin{infobox}{Second Law}
+The acceleration of an object is directly proportional to the net force acting on it, and inversely proportional to its mass.
+\[
+  \boxed{F = m \times a}
+  \qquad\qquad
+  \boxed{a = \frac{F}{m}}
+  \qquad\qquad
+  \boxed{m = \frac{F}{a}}
+\]
+Force in Newtons (N) \quad Mass in kilograms (kg) \quad Acceleration in m/s$^2$
+\end{infobox}
+
+\begin{workedex}{Worked Example 2.1 --- Finding acceleration}
+A 5\,kg trolley is pushed with a net force of 20\,N. What is its acceleration?
+
+\medskip
+Write the formula: \quad $F = m \times a$ \quad $\Rightarrow$ \quad $a = F \div m$
+
+Substitute: \quad $a = 20 \div 5 = \mathbf{4\ \text{m/s}^2}$
+\end{workedex}
+
+\begin{yr7box}
+\textbf{Task 2.2} \markscount{6}\quad Use $F = ma$ to complete the table. Show working in the space below.
+
