+  <section class="slide title-slide active" data-menu="Title">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Year 7 Science <b>&#183;</b> Forces &amp; Motion <b>&#183;</b> Lesson 1 of 6</p>
+    <h1>What is a Force?</h1>
+    <p class="subtitle">Types of force, free body diagrams, and balanced vs unbalanced forces.</p>
+    <div class="head-meta">
+      <span class="pill">Unit: Forces &amp; Motion</span>
+      <span class="pill">Year 7&#8211;8</span>
+      <span class="pill">Booklet Section 1</span>
+    </div>
+    <p class="start-hint">Press <span class="key">&#8594;</span> to begin.</p>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Learning intentions">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Section 1 <b>&#183;</b> Overview</p>
+    <h2>What we&#8217;re learning today</h2>
+    <div class="cols">
+      <div class="box key"><span class="lbl">Learning intentions</span>
+        <ul>
+          <li>Understand that a force is a push or a pull, measured in newtons</li>
+          <li>Distinguish contact from non-contact forces</li>
+          <li>Represent forces using free body diagrams</li>
+          <li>Explain balanced and unbalanced forces using net force</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+      <div class="box try"><span class="lbl">Success criteria</span>
+        <ul>
+          <li>I can classify forces as contact or non-contact</li>
+          <li>I can draw and label a free body diagram</li>
+          <li>I can calculate net force and predict motion</li>
+          <li>I can explain why an object does or doesn&#8217;t accelerate</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Starter discussion</span><p>Think of three forces acting on you right now. Which ones involve something touching you?</p></div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Defining force">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet pp.2&#8211;3 &#183; Every force is a push or a pull</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Concept</p>
+    <h2>A force is a push or a pull</h2>
+    <div class="cols">
+      <div>
+        <p>Forces are measured in <strong>newtons (N)</strong>, named after Sir Isaac Newton. A force can:</p>
+        <ul>
+          <li>Start or stop a moving object</li>
+          <li>Change the speed of motion</li>
+          <li>Change the direction of motion</li>
+          <li>Change the shape of an object</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+      <div>
+        <div class="box key"><span class="lbl">Key idea</span><p>Forces always act <strong>in a direction</strong>. We show this with arrows &#8212; the longer the arrow, the bigger the force.</p></div>
+        <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Check for understanding</span><p>If you push a shopping trolley and it speeds up, which two of the four effects are happening?</p></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Types of force">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet p.6 &#183; Types of force</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Concept</p>
+    <h2>Contact and non-contact forces</h2>
+    <div class="graph-card narrow">
+      <svg viewBox="0 20 900 270" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
+        <rect x="20" y="30" width="410" height="245" rx="14" stroke-width="2.5" style="fill:var(--card);stroke:var(--accent)"/>
+        <text x="225" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26" font-weight="700" font-family="Bitter, serif" style="fill:var(--accent-dark)">Contact forces</text>
+        <text x="225" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" style="fill:var(--ink-soft)">objects must be touching</text>
+        <text x="60" y="140" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Friction</text>
+        <text x="60" y="172" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Normal force</text>
+        <text x="60" y="204" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Tension</text>
+        <text x="60" y="236" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Air resistance</text>
+        <text x="240" y="140" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Applied force</text>
+        <text x="240" y="172" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Spring force</text>
+        <rect x="470" y="30" width="410" height="245" rx="14" stroke-width="2.5" style="fill:var(--accent-warm-soft);stroke:var(--accent-warm)"/>
+        <text x="675" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26" font-weight="700" font-family="Bitter, serif" style="fill:var(--ink)">Non-contact forces</text>
+        <text x="675" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" style="fill:var(--ink-soft)">act at a distance</text>
+        <text x="510" y="140" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Gravity</text>
+        <text x="510" y="172" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Magnetic force</text>
+        <text x="510" y="204" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Electrostatic force</text>
+        <text x="510" y="236" font-size="20" style="fill:var(--ink)">&#9679; Nuclear force</text>
+      </svg>
+    </div>
+    <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Discuss</span><p>A magnet picks up a paperclip without touching it. Contact or non-contact? What about the friction that stops the paperclip sliding off your desk?</p></div>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- FREE BODY DIAGRAMS — proportional arrows, scale 4px/N.
+       Force-arrow hues are content (each force keeps its own colour so
+       students can tell them apart) and stay literal; chrome is tokens. -->
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Free body diagrams">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet p.7 &#183; Free body diagrams</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Concept</p>
+    <h2>Free body diagrams</h2>
+    <div class="graph-card narrow">
+      <svg viewBox="0 8 900 304" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
+        <text x="220" y="32" text-anchor="middle" font-size="21" font-weight="700" font-family="Bitter, serif" style="fill:var(--ink)">Balanced &#8212; no acceleration</text>
+        <line x1="100" y1="210" x2="340" y2="210" stroke-width="2.5" style="stroke:var(--ink-faint)"/>
+        <rect x="180" y="168" width="80" height="42" rx="6" style="fill:var(--accent)"/>
+        <text x="220" y="194" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#fff" font-weight="600">Book</text>
+        <line x1="220" y1="168" x2="220" y2="128" stroke="#18A999" stroke-width="5"/>
+        <polygon points="220,118 213,134 227,134" fill="#18A999"/>
+        <text x="232" y="140" font-size="16" fill="#0F6E63" font-weight="600">N = 10 N</text>
+        <line x1="220" y1="210" x2="220" y2="250" stroke="#E0533F" stroke-width="5"/>
+        <polygon points="220,260 213,244 227,244" fill="#E0533F"/>
+        <text x="232" y="240" font-size="16" fill="#B23A2B" font-weight="600">W = 10 N</text>
+        <text x="220" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-style="italic" style="fill:var(--ink-soft)">arrows equal length &#8594; forces balanced</text>
+
+        <text x="660" y="32" text-anchor="middle" font-size="21" font-weight="700" font-family="Bitter, serif" style="fill:var(--ink)">Unbalanced &#8212; accelerates &#8594;</text>
+        <line x1="470" y1="210" x2="850" y2="210" stroke-width="2.5" style="stroke:var(--ink-faint)"/>
+        <rect x="600" y="168" width="80" height="42" rx="6" style="fill:var(--accent)"/>
+        <text x="640" y="194" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#fff" font-weight="600">Box</text>
+        <line x1="680" y1="189" x2="760" y2="189" stroke="#7C5CE0" stroke-width="5"/>
+        <polygon points="770,189 754,182 754,196" fill="#7C5CE0"/>
+        <text x="690" y="160" font-size="16" fill="#5B3FC0" font-weight="600">Push = 20 N</text>
+        <line x1="600" y1="189" x2="580" y2="189" stroke="#D98A0B" stroke-width="5"/>
+        <polygon points="570,189 586,182 586,196" fill="#D98A0B"/>
+        <text x="460" y="160" font-size="16" fill="#A86A08" font-weight="600">Friction = 5 N</text>
+        <text x="660" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-style="italic" style="fill:var(--ink-soft)">push arrow 4&#215; longer than friction &#8594; net 15 N right</text>
+      </svg>
+    </div>
+    <div class="box defn"><span class="lbl">Definition</span><p>A <strong>free body diagram</strong> shows every force on one object as a labelled arrow. <strong>Arrow length is proportional to the size of the force</strong> &#8212; a 20&nbsp;N arrow is twice as long as a 10&nbsp;N arrow.</p></div>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- DRAW IT — model answer is a .frag, so → reveals it before advancing -->
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Draw it: skydiver">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet p.8 &#183; Task 1.2c</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Draw it &#8212; whole class or in books</p>
+    <h2><span class="phase-tag youdo">You do</span> Draw the free body diagram</h2>
+    <div class="cols">
+      <div>
+        <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Skydiver at terminal velocity</span><p>A <strong>skydiver</strong> has reached <strong>terminal velocity</strong> &#8212; falling at a steady speed. Draw and label the free body diagram. Make your arrow <strong>lengths match the sizes</strong> of the forces.</p></div>
+        <p class="note">Let students draw first, then press &#8594; to reveal the model answer.</p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="graph-card narrow frag">
+        <svg viewBox="0 0 360 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
+          <text x="12" y="24" font-size="15" font-weight="700" style="fill:var(--neg)">&#10003; Model answer</text>
+          <circle cx="180" cy="150" r="26" style="fill:var(--accent)"/>
+          <text x="180" y="156" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#fff" font-weight="600">diver</text>
+          <line x1="180" y1="176" x2="180" y2="256" stroke="#E0533F" stroke-width="6"/>
+          <polygon points="180,268 171,250 189,250" fill="#E0533F"/>
+          <text x="196" y="230" font-size="17" fill="#B23A2B" font-weight="600">Weight</text>
+          <line x1="180" y1="124" x2="180" y2="44" stroke="#18A999" stroke-width="6"/>
+          <polygon points="180,32 171,50 189,50" fill="#18A999"/>
+          <text x="196" y="80" font-size="17" fill="#0F6E63" font-weight="600">Air resistance</text>
+          <text x="180" y="292" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-style="italic" style="fill:var(--ink-soft)">equal arrows &#8594; balanced &#8594; constant speed</text>
+        </svg>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Worked example: net force">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet p.7 &#183; Task 1.2b</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Work through with the class</p>
+    <h2><span class="phase-tag ido">I do</span> Worked example &#8212; net force</h2>
+    <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Find the net force and the motion</span><p>A box on a smooth floor is pushed right with <strong>20&nbsp;N</strong>. Friction pushes left with <strong>5&nbsp;N</strong>. What is the net force, and what happens to the box?</p></div>
+    <ol class="steps" id="f1w1">
+      <li><span class="stepno">1</span> Identify the two horizontal forces: push \(= 20\ \text{N}\) right, friction \(= 5\ \text{N}\) left.</li>
+      <li><span class="stepno">2</span> The forces act in opposite directions, so <strong>subtract</strong> them.</li>
+      <li><span class="stepno">3</span> \(F_{\text{net}} = 20 - 5 = 15\ \text{N}\), pointing right.</li>
+      <li class="ans"><span class="stepno">&#10003;</span> <span class="result">The forces are unbalanced, so the box accelerates to the right.</span></li>
+    </ol>
+    <button class="reveal-btn" data-target="#f1w1">Show working</button>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Your turn">
+    <p class="bookref book">Booklet p.8 &#183; Extension 1.3</p>
+    <p class="eyebrow">Your turn</p>
+    <h2><span class="phase-tag youdo">You do</span> Now you try</h2>
+    <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Predict the motion of the car</span><p>A car has a driving force of <strong>4500&nbsp;N</strong> forward. Friction and air resistance total <strong>1200&nbsp;N</strong> backward. Find the net force and describe the car&#8217;s motion.</p></div>
+    <ol class="steps" id="f1w2">
+      <li><span class="stepno">1</span> Opposite directions, so subtract: \(4500 - 1200\).</li>
+      <li><span class="stepno">2</span> \(F_{\text{net}} = 3300\ \text{N}\) forward.</li>
+      <li class="ans"><span class="stepno">&#10003;</span> <span class="result">Unbalanced force &#8212; the car accelerates forward (speeds up).</span></li>
+    </ol>
+    <button class="reveal-btn" data-target="#f1w2">Show answers</button>
+  </section>
+
+  <!-- LAB POINTER — repoint at experiments.html#exp1 once that page is ported -->
+  <section class="slide section-slide" data-menu="&#8594; Lab: Experiment 1">
+    <p class="kicker">Lab time</p>
+    <h2>Experiment 1 &#8212; Investigating friction</h2>
+    <p>Time to head into the lab. The aim, equipment, method and results table are in the student booklet &#8212; open Experiment 1 to run the activity.</p>
+    <a class="chip" href="booklet/Forces_Motion_Unit_Booklet_Yr7_8.pdf#page=11">Open Experiment 1 in the booklet &#8594;</a>
+    <p class="slide-foot">Booklet pp.11&#8211;13 &#183; How does the type of surface affect the friction force?</p>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="slide" data-menu="Recap">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Section 1 <b>&#183;</b> Recap</p>
+    <h2>What we covered</h2>
+    <ul>
+      <li>A <strong>force</strong> is a push or pull, measured in newtons (N)</li>
+      <li><strong>Contact</strong> forces need touching; <strong>non-contact</strong> forces act at a distance</li>
+      <li><strong>Free body diagrams</strong> show all forces as arrows, with <strong>length proportional to size</strong></li>
+      <li><strong>Balanced</strong> forces &#8594; no acceleration; <strong>unbalanced</strong> &#8594; acceleration</li>
+      <li><strong>Net force</strong> is the overall force after combining all the forces</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="box question"><span class="lbl">Exit question</span><p>A book sits still on a table. Are the forces on it balanced or unbalanced? How do you know?</p></div>
+  </section>
+
+</main>
